a1_riddle.text:
{{#if lied}}{{#if debt>=1}}He straightens a glove and looks, if anything, pleased. "Then we are playing after all, and you are our visitor."{{/if}}{{/if}} Further in, the waltz stops on one held note, as if the house were drawing breath. "While the house sees to things," says your host, "perhaps a small game?"

"I have a face and two hands, and I have not moved since the night a boy came to the door. What am I?"{{#if visits>0}} He asks it exactly as he asked it before, and does not seem to mind that you know.{{/if}}

He waits. The portraits, the coats and the clocks all hold very still, as if they would like to hear the answer too.

a2_hub.text:
{{#if riddle=right}}"The clock," your host agrees, absently touching his watch chain.{{else}}Your host considers your answer. "A good guess," he says, which is not the same as a right one.{{/if}}

Now that you are through the arch, the house sets out its doors for you. To your left a pair of panelled ones stands ajar, and through the gap you can hear a fire burning. From a passage on the right comes the smell of bread and cloves. The stair is there too, broad and carpeted, and a velvet rope hangs across its foot.

"You will not have time for all of them," he says, quite plainly.{{#if visits>0}} You never have.{{/if}} {{#if stance=treat}}"Do say, my guest, if there is anything you need," he adds.{{else}}{{#if riddle=right}}"One point to our visitor," he announces to the chandelier.{{else}}"One point to the house," he announces to the chandelier.{{/if}}{{/if}}

a2_kitchen_thanks.text:
"Thank you, Raissa," you say.

She stands with her hands caught in her apron and does not move, the way a person keeps still when a bird has landed on them. {{#if stance=treat}}Then she takes the wishbone off its saucer by the range and folds your fingers over it with her own, which are warm and very dry.{{else}}Then, still without turning round, she reaches behind her, takes the wishbone off its saucer by the range, and puts it in your hand.{{/if}} "That's not on his books," she says. "A thank-you isn't." Nowhere in the house does a pen lift.

{{#if stance=treat}}Already, because she cannot help it, she is reaching for a plate. "You'll have a little something, though, duck."{{else}}"I still don't serve strangers," she tells the wall, and takes the lid off the pot, and leaves it off.{{/if}}

a2_cons_lesson.text:
Eric leans in, though there is nobody to hear but leaves. "Here's how you fool it." He taps the dish twice. "It can't call a lie a lie, man. It's too polite. Whatever you tell it, it has to take your word and smile and go 'Ah.'"

{{#if stance=treat}}Then he taps again, twice, and the grin goes. "But a guest who lies stops being a guest. Once you lie, nothing in here has to be nice to you."{{else}}"Next time the old man asks you something, give him the answer you'd like to be true," he says. "He'll thank you for it." His fingers stay still on the dish.{{/if}}{{#if lied}} He squints at you. "You already lied, didn't you. Natural."{{/if}}

All round the little circle of his light, the vines have crept closer to listen. He holds the dish out to you again.

a3_nursery_pip.text:
"I'm Dee," she says. "Dina Ashdown, really, but nobody says it." Then she asks the thing she has been waiting to ask. "Is it still Halloween out there? I said I'd be home before Mum switches the porch light off. I've only been here about an hour."

The little clock above the crib says one minute to midnight, like every clock in the house. {{#if riddle=right}}"The house says you guessed the clock downstairs," she says. "So you'll know how late it really is."{{else}}"They all say that," she says. "Mr. Marrow says they're only being polite, so as not to hurry anybody."{{/if}}

Inside the wall, low down by the skirting, something knocks three times, and waits for an answer too.

a3_nursery_year.text:
When you tell her, the sheet goes still. Then her fingers begin to move under it, counting. She gets past her own age, and past her mother's, and has to go back and start again, and then she gets there and stops.

"That's not an hour," she says.

She does not cry. She goes to the window instead and kneels on the chair with her eyeholes to the glass. {{#if fed}}Where she looks you see only a grey nothing, and she looks at it a long time.{{else}}At the bottom of a street that runs on too far, a porch light in a little iron cage is still on.{{/if}}

{{#if trust>=2}}Then she unties a ribbon from under the sheet and holds it out to you. "You'd better take me, then. Promise properly. Promises count at midnight; everyone knows that."{{else}}She winds a ribbon round her fingers, as if she might give it to you, and does not hold it out, not yet.{{/if}}

a3_attic_stair.text:
The attic steps climb into the sound of rain. Under the eaves, trunks stand in rows with a year chalked on every lid, 1931 and 1953 and 1979 and dozens more.

The oldest says 1898. Inside are a boy's Sunday clothes and a cork burnt black at one end, the same black as the moustache on your host's lip. A paper tag is pinned inside the lid. {{#if candle}}Your flame leans toward it as though it wanted to read it too.{{else}}{{#if ledger}}Its capitals lean to the right, the way the 1898 line leans in the guestbook.{{else}}In the house's light it is a brown smudge of careful capitals, a name, almost.{{/if}}{{/if}}{{#unless name_given}}

Under the floorboards a girl's voice says, "I'm meant to have a room downstairs. Tonight there isn't one, so I'm under here. Is it still Halloween out there?"{{/unless}}

a3_attic_floor.text:
Rain goes over the roof like someone counting coins. Then, under it, three knocks come up through the attic floor, slow and evenly spaced, from so far down that the whole house ought to be between them and the soles of your feet. The boards under your feet are warm.{{#unless name_given}} The girl under the boards has heard them too. "Don't," she whispers. "It's only asking. But if you answer, that's you saying yes."{{/unless}}{{#if knocks>=1}}

Cloves rise between the boards, and the floor says "Welcome." It has borrowed a girl's voice for the word, small and polite, without asking her.{{/if}}

After that, nothing comes up through the boards but the warmth. Whatever is down there is very good at waiting.

a4_cellar.text:
The cellar is as warm as the air in front of an oven door. Roots come through the walls, pale as the inside of a loaf, and they have pushed the bricks aside so gently that the mortar is still whole. Every one of them leans the same way, like weeds in a current, toward something further in. {{#if fed}}They smell of bread and cloves, and though you have eaten in this house, your mouth waters.{{else}}They smell of bread and cloves, the way a kitchen smells on a feast day.{{/if}} Along one wall, shelves of labelled jars run off into the dark.{{#if trust>=1}}

A sheet with crooked eyeholes slips past you, light as laundry, and looks back from between the roots. "This way," Dee whispers. "They all go to the door. Don't touch the jars."{{else}}{{#if trust<=-2}}

The roots draw apart before you reach them, the way a crowd parts for someone it has been told about.{{/if}}{{/if}}

a4_jars_sweets.text:
The lid comes off with a small, reluctant sound, and you tip the sweets into your palm. The waxed paper crackles when you close your hand.{{#if stance=treat}} They are the same sweets the gloved hand gave you at the door, twisted the same way at both ends.{{/if}}{{#if trust>=0}}

The sheet beside you does not reach for them. "I was saving those," Dee says. Her crooked eyeholes stay on your hand, and she waits, expectantly.{{else}}

Nobody down here minds. On the shelf the empty jar begins to turn by itself, slowly, once round and then once more, looking for what used to be in it.{{/if}}

a4_gate.text:
Mr. Marrow closes the green book and comes round to hold your chair. Behind him hangs a plain clock, its hands at one minute to midnight.{{#if clock}} It is ticking, and he does not look round at it.{{/if}}{{#if riddle=right}} "It stopped the night I said Treat," he says, seeing you look. "You were the first to guess."{{/if}}

Then the cloth lifts, every candle leans toward the middle of the table, and from under it something speaks its one word. "Welcome." It uses your voice, and wears it carefully, like borrowed shoes.{{#if knocks>=3}} It is close now, under the boards beneath your chair, waiting to be asked up.{{/if}}

He does not tell you what to do. He never would.

a4_gate_name.text:
"Douglas A. Tull," you say.

It goes into the room like a stone into a well, and the table goes still to hear where it lands. "Ashby," he says. For a moment he is not Mr. Marrow but a boy of eleven in his dead father's coat, hearing his mother call him in. He wipes his mouth with a glove, and the burnt-cork moustache smears. "Douglas Ashby Tull. Yes. One question, or one order, and I shall have to. Make it something good."

{{#if fed}}His eyes go to your hands and stay there a moment, the way a tailor's eyes measure a sleeve.{{else}}You have eaten nothing in this house, and the way home is still where you left it, waiting for a door.{{/if}}{{#if ledger}} You think of the guestbook's first line, blank since 1898, where a host would sign.{{/if}}

end_paid_in_full.text:
"The bill," you say. "Please."

Mr. Marrow takes a slip from the back of the green book and turns it round beside your plate: *Oct. 31*, and ruled columns. In the column marked *Item* there is one line. {{#if name_given}}The line is your name.{{else}}It is a blank the length of a name.{{/if}} {{#if debt>=1}}What you owed is listed down the side, and every line is crossed through, settled with what you were.{{else}}Under *Owing* there is nothing at all, and that is worse: you have no claim here on a chair, a crumb or a door, so nothing stops the house from keeping you.{{#unless clock}} "A guest's clean page is let out at midnight," he says. "Midnight did not come."{{/unless}}{{/if}}

He closes the book and says, gently, "Lovely." Whatever was on that line goes down into the warm dark, and the table is one guest fuller.
